            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-7877ad5b1e0eb48131b4\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"The Battle of Gettysburg was a three-day Civil War clash fought near Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, July 1–3, 1863. Confederate General Robert E. Lee had moved north into Pennsylvania seeking supplies and a victory that might pressure the United States to make peace; Union forces under General George G. Meade met his army there. After fierce fighting around hills and ridges, the Union held its defensive line against the July 3 assault known as Pickett’s Charge. Lee withdrew toward Virginia beginning July 4, ending his invasion campaign in defeat but not the Civil War. On November 19, Lincoln delivered the Gettysburg Address at the dedication of a cemetery for Union dead.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm\",\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm\",\"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/gettysburg-address/\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"Lee’s move into Pennsylvania aimed partly to find supplies and win a victory that could increase pressure on the U.S. government to seek peace.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"The battle began on July 1, when Confederate and Union forces met near Gettysburg; Union troops were pushed through town and regrouped on high ground south of it.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm\",\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"The Union defensive position across Cemetery Ridge, Cemetery Hill, Culp’s Hill, and Little Round Top formed a line often described as a fishhook.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"On July 3, Pickett’s Charge attacked the Union center on Cemetery Ridge and failed; Lee began retreating to Virginia the next day.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm\",\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"Lincoln delivered the Gettysburg Address on November 19, 1863, at the dedication of the Soldiers’ National Cemetery; the war continued after the battle.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/gettysburg-address/\",\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Gettysburg Overview\",\"url\":\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm\",\"publisher\":\"Gettysburg National Military Park, National Park Service\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:54:44.263194+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Battle dates, opposing commanders, sequence of fighting, Pickett’s Charge, Lee’s retreat, the Gettysburg Address, and the fact that the Civil War continued.\"},{\"title\":\"Essential Background: The Battle of Gettysburg\",\"url\":\"https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm\",\"publisher\":\"Gettysburg National Military Park, National Park Service\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:54:44.263194+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Lee’s campaign aims, the first day’s fighting, the Union’s fishhook-shaped defensive line, and the failed July 3 assault.\"},{\"title\":\"Gettysburg Address\",\"url\":\"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/gettysburg-address/\",\"publisher\":\"Library of Congress\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:54:44.263194+00:00\",\"supports\":\"The date and cemetery-dedication setting of Lincoln’s Gettysburg Address.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:nps.gov Gettysburg battle July 1 3 1863 overview\",\"site:loc.gov Gettysburg battle July 1863 Lincoln address history\",\"site:history.army.mil Gettysburg battle overview July 1863\",\"site:archives.gov Gettysburg Address November 19 1863 Lincoln cemetery\",\"site:loc.gov Gettysburg Address Lincoln delivered November 19 1863 cemetery\",\"site:army.mil Gettysburg campaign battle July 1 3 1863 Lee Meade\"],\"notes\":\"The overview is 109 words. The overview and facts draw on directly opened National Park Service and Library of Congress pages. The failed Library of Congress guide and inaccessible Army history page were not used.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
