    "overview": {
      "text": "The Battle of Gettysburg was a three-day Civil War clash fought near Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, July 1–3, 1863. Confederate General Robert E. Lee had moved north into Pennsylvania seeking supplies and a victory that might pressure the United States to make peace; Union forces under General George G. Meade met his army there. After fierce fighting around hills and ridges, the Union held its defensive line against the July 3 assault known as Pickett’s Charge. Lee withdrew toward Virginia beginning July 4, ending his invasion campaign in defeat but not the Civil War. On November 19, Lincoln delivered the Gettysburg Address at the dedication of a cemetery for Union dead.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm",
        "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm",
        "https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/gettysburg-address/"
      ]
    },
    "key_facts": [
      {
        "text": "Lee’s move into Pennsylvania aimed partly to find supplies and win a victory that could increase pressure on the U.S. government to seek peace.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The battle began on July 1, when Confederate and Union forces met near Gettysburg; Union troops were pushed through town and regrouped on high ground south of it.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm",
          "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The Union defensive position across Cemetery Ridge, Cemetery Hill, Culp’s Hill, and Little Round Top formed a line often described as a fishhook.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "On July 3, Pickett’s Charge attacked the Union center on Cemetery Ridge and failed; Lee began retreating to Virginia the next day.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm",
          "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/education/classrooms/essential-background-the-battle-of-gettysburg.htm"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Lincoln delivered the Gettysburg Address on November 19, 1863, at the dedication of the Soldiers’ National Cemetery; the war continued after the battle.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/gettysburg-address/",
          "https://www.nps.gov/gett/learn/historyculture/gettysburg-overview.htm"
        ]
